": 0.9816436171531677, "span": {"begin": 1160, "end": 1163}}, {"id": ["mesh:D014280"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "triglycerides", "obj": "drug", "prob": 0.9860588908195496, "span": {"begin": 1207, "end": 1220}}, {"id": ["mesh:D010900"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "Endothelial dysfunction", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9901280403137207, "span": {"begin": 1294, "end": 1317}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6287"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SAA", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9443452954292297, "span": {"begin": 1395, "end": 1398}}, {"id": ["mesh:D010900"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "endothelial dysfunction", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9994586706161499, "span": {"begin": 1424, "end": 1447}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:948"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "CD36", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9863081574440002, "span": {"begin": 1468, "end": 1472}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6288", "6289"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "SAA receptor", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9948477149009705, "span": {"begin": 1482, "end": 1494}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "macrophages", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.64188551902771, "span": {"begin": 1508, "end": 1519}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "endothelial cells", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.59737628698349, "span": {"begin": 1524, "end": 1541}}, {"id": ["NCBITaxon:9606"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "human", "obj": "species", "prob": 0.9959099292755127, "span": {"begin": 1604, "end": 1609}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6287"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SAA", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9922705888748169, "span": {"begin": 1632, "end": 1635}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1401"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "C-reactive protein", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9982092380523682, "span": {"begin": 1640, "end": 1658}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1401"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "CRP", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9961071014404297, "span": {"begin": 1660, "end": 1663}}, {"id": ["NCBITaxon:9606"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "humans", "obj": "species", "prob": 0.9874424338340759, "span": {"begin": 1710, "end": 1716}}, {"id": ["mesh:D002318"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "cardiovascular", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.688728928565979, "span": {"begin": 1841, "end": 1855}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1401"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "CRP gene", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9961473345756531, "span": {"begin": 1967, "end": 1975}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1401"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "CRP", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9922688603401184, "span": {"begin": 1991, "end": 1994}}, {"id": ["mesh:D003327", "mim:300464"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "coronary heart disease", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9999983906745911, "span": {"begin": 2024, "end": 2046}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1401"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "CRP", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9947350025177002, "span": {"begin": 2118, "end": 2121}}, {"id": ["mesh:D003327", "mim:300464"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "coronary heart disease", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9999990463256836, "span": {"begin": 2157, "end": 2179}}], "text": "The biological plausibility is high. The acute phase response promotes atherosclerosis in several ways:    SAA directly promotes foam cell formation. During acute phase response, SAA, one of the major acute phase proteins, replaces apolipoprotein A-1 in high density lipoprotein (HDL). This replacement obstructs the reverse transport of cholesterol to the liver, allowing the accumulation of cholesterol in macrophages, turning them into foam cells (Lindhorst, Young, Bagshaw, Hyland, &amp; Kisilevsky, 1997; McGillicuddy et al., 2009; Meek, Urieli-Shoval, &amp; Benditt, 1994).  Induction of acute phase response affects cholesterol homeostasis. Cholesterol synthesis mainly takes place in the liver and cholesterol circulates in the blood as part of HDL and low density lipoprotein (LDL) molecular complexes. In mice, induction of the acute phase response leads to profound changes in cholesterol biosynthesis and in blood levels of lipids (Bourdon et al., 2012; Lindhorst et al., 1997; Saber et al., 2014). When humans undergo acute phase response, LDL synthesis is increased, but LDL levels in blood decrease due to upregulation of LDL receptor activity. HDL blood levels decrease, and blood levels of triglycerides increase (Balci, 2011; Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999; Saber et al., 2014).   Endothelial dysfunction has been proposed as a pro-atherosclerotic mechanism of particle inhalation. SAA has been shown to induce endothelial dysfunction by interaction with CD36, &nbsp;a SAA receptor expressed on macrophages and endothelial cells (Robertson et al., 2013; Wang et al., 2008).    The two major human acute phase response, SAA and C-reactive protein (CRP), have been shown to be closely correlated in humans (Baumann et al., 2018; Monse et al., 2018; Ridker, Hennekens, Buring, &amp; Rifai, 2000), and both are biomarkers of future cardiovascular event risks (Ridker et al., 2000). However, Mendelian randomization studies show that genetic variation in the CRP gene correlate with CRP levels, but not with risk of coronary heart disease (Collaboration et al., 2011; Elliott et al., 2009). This suggests that CRP levels are not causally related to coronary heart disease, but correlate closely with the causal agent, which could be SAA.", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 15:49:42 +0000 2026"}